I’ve been spending some time looking beyond just writing Terraform code and understanding what actually happens behind the scenes.

One thing that became much clearer to me is how Terraform, state, and configuration management tools like Ansible fit together.

🔹 How Terraform keeps track of infrastructure

Terraform communicates with the cloud provider through the provider plugin.

For example, when we define an EC2 instance, VPC, GKE cluster, or any other resource in our .tf files, Terraform communicates with the provider and creates or manages that resource.

Terraform also maintains a state file to keep track of the infrastructure it manages.

The important point is:

.tf configuration → desired state
State file → Terraform's record of managed infrastructure
Provider → actual infrastructure

So, what happens if someone manually deletes a resource from the cloud console?

During the next Terraform operation, Terraform refreshes its knowledge of the infrastructure and detects that the actual infrastructure no longer matches what is defined in the configuration.

Terraform can then determine what needs to be created or changed to bring the infrastructure back to the desired state.

That is one of the things I really like about Infrastructure as Code — the configuration becomes repeatable instead of relying on manual changes.

🔹 Where Ansible comes in

Terraform is excellent at provisioning infrastructure.

But after the server is created, we may still need to:

Install packages

Create users

Configure services

Deploy configuration files

Run application setup

Apply OS-level configuration

This is where Ansible can complement Terraform.

A common approach is:

Terraform → Create infrastructure → Get server information → Ansible → Configure servers

For example, Terraform can create multiple servers and expose their IP addresses through outputs. Those IPs can then be used to generate or provide an Ansible inventory.

Ansible can then take over the configuration management part.

🔹 Data Sources

Another Terraform feature I find very useful is data sources.

Data sources allow Terraform to retrieve information about resources that already exist, without Terraform necessarily managing those resources.

For example, instead of creating a VPC, Terraform can query an existing VPC and use its ID when creating other resources.

This becomes especially useful when working with existing infrastructure.

🔹 Locals

Locals are another small but useful Terraform feature.

They allow us to assign a name to an expression or calculated value and reuse it throughout the configuration.

Unlike variables, locals aren't intended to be overridden through input values.

For example, instead of repeating the same naming expression in multiple places, we can calculate it once in a local and reuse it.

It makes larger Terraform configurations easier to read and maintain.

🔹 Terraform Provisioners

Terraform also provides provisioners such as:

local-exec → runs a command on the machine running Terraform

remote-exec → connects to a remote machine and executes commands

file → copies files to a remote machine

They can be useful for specific tasks after infrastructure creation.

However, I prefer using provisioners carefully and using purpose-built tools like Ansible for configuration management when possible.

🔹 Terraform + Ansible

For me, the biggest takeaway is that Terraform and Ansible don't necessarily compete with each other.

They solve different parts of the infrastructure problem.

Terraform → Infrastructure provisioning
Ansible → Configuration management

Once these responsibilities are separated, the overall automation becomes much easier to understand and maintain.

These may look like small Terraform concepts — state, data sources, locals, provisioners — but understanding how they work together makes a big difference when working with real infrastructure.

